            "{'_id': ObjectId('573a13d6f29313caabd9fe6f'), 'plot': 'Keshya, a simple villager believes that god has arrived in his village but everyone disbelieves him. Later when the politics plays its part things take a drastic change.', 'genres': ['Drama'], 'runtime': 135, 'cast': ['Mohan Agashe', 'Vibhawari Deshpande', 'Hrishikesh Joshi', 'Abhijeet Khaire'], 'poster': 'https://m.media-amazon.com/images/M/MV5BMjI0Mzg3MTE3MV5BMl5BanBnXkFtZTcwNjc4NTU3Nw@@._V1_SY1000_SX677_AL_.jpg', 'title': 'The Temple', 'fullplot': \"In the rural areas of Maharashtra lies a peaceful village called Mangrul. One day Keshav (Girish Kulkarni), a village youth, sees lord Dattatrey (Datta) in his dreams while taking a nap under a tree. He makes a hue and cry in the village saying God made an appearance for him. Anna (Dilip Prabhavalkar), most respected figure of Mangrul, advises him against announcing such personal matter as it's a question of faith. However, it is too late as a journalist (Kishor Kadam) sensationalizes the news about Lord Dattatrey making an appearance in Mangrul. There is now a demand for a Dattatrey temple. Bhau (Nana Patekar), a political activist, doesn't approve it as he wants the funds to be used for better purposes but he seems helpless. The temple is built and the village becomes a holy place. Mangrul goes through a 360 degree change due to commercialization but nobody is complaining except Anna. Soon, blinded by the commercial progress, God is forgotten. Every village has a right to progress commercially but how ethical it is to use a temple and its God to achieve it? The question is raised by Writer Girish Kulkarni & Director Umesh Vinayak Kulkarni's latest offering 'Deool', without being judgemental. While it does this, the audience is treated to some quality cinematic experience which is enlightening.\", 'languages': ['Marathi'], 'released': datetime.datetime(2011, 9, 15, 0, 0), 'directors': ['Umesh Vinayak Kulkarni'], 'writers': ['Girish Kulkarni (screenpl