            "{'_id': ObjectId('573a13e8f29313caabdc9ad6'), 'plot': \"Greyhawks Rugby Club is under threat from land developers, and Dave's position as chairman is being challenged by an extremely loud Australian. The faithful few have gathered on the morning...\", 'genres': ['Comedy'], 'runtime': 95, 'cast': ['Michael Beckley', 'Beth Cordingly', 'Chris England', 'Nigel Lindsay'], 'poster': 'https://m.media-amazon.com/images/M/MV5BMTQ5ODUwMzI0MV5BMl5BanBnXkFtZTgwNzYzNTY1MDE@._V1_SY1000_SX677_AL_.jpg', 'title': 'Breakfast with Jonny Wilkinson', 'fullplot': \"Greyhawks Rugby Club is under threat from land developers, and Dave's position as chairman is being challenged by an extremely loud Australian. The faithful few have gathered on the morning before the election to watch England in the Rugby World Cup Final and tensions are running high for more reasons than one Why does Jake, the first team prodigy, leave the room every time Jonny Wilkinson takes a kick? Why has Dave stopped drinking? And why have only seven people turned up to watch the game of the century? This sparky and engaging comedy returns to that golden morning in 2003 when the unthinkable happened and England were victorious.\", 'languages': ['English'], 'released': datetime.datetime(2013, 11, 22, 0, 0), 'directors': ['Simon Sprackling'], 'writers': ['Chris England'], 'awards': {'wins': 0, 'nominations': 1, 'text': '1 nomination.'}, 'lastupdated': '2015-08-05 00:30:54.117000000', 'year': 2013, 'imdb': {'rating': 5.4, 'votes': 34, 'id': 3198544}, 'countries': ['UK'], 'type': 'movie', 'num_mflix_comments': 0}\n",
            "{'_id': ObjectId('573a13e8f29313caabdc9cf6'), 'plot': 'Alberta singer/songwriter Rae Spoon reflects on gender identity, faith, and the challenges of family through songs and stories.', 'genres': ['Documentary', 'Music'], 'runtime': 76, 'cast': ['Rae Spoon'], 'num_mflix_comments': 1, 'poster': 'https://m.media-amazon.com/images/M/MV5BNDhmYjFkYmMtODIxMy00ZDQ2LWEyMmEtOTA5MDA5NWYzYThjXkEyXkFqcGdeQXVyNTA1NDY3NzY@._V1_SY1000_SX677_AL_.jpg', 'title': 'My Prairie Home', 'fullplot': 'Canadian indie-folk musician Rae Spoon is followed on a concert tour via Greyhound bus through the prairies where they (a self-proclaimed pronoun as he or she are inadequate) grew up. Theirs is a life of dichotomies: being born biologically female, but identifying emotionally as male (hence the reason they does not identify as being a lesbian anymore); having what they describe as a difficult growing up period in the prairies in an evangelically Christian family with a tyrant father, yet embracing their past on the prairies as a shared history which has shaped the person they is; and pursuing a music career, a public oriented career, while realizing that being in the public eye opens them up to potentially dangerous personal situations, not only for them but also their girlfriend (their high school sweetheart), especially on the conservative prairies because of their transgendered status (which is why they assumed the gender neutral name Rae). Through this storytelling, Spoon performs their music, which are highly personal stories of their diverse life.', 'languages': ['English'], 'released': datetime.datetime(2014, 1, 17, 0, 0), 'directors': ['Chelsea McMullan'], 'writers': ['Chelsea McMullan'], 'awards': {'wins': 3, 'nominations': 3, 'text': '3 wins & 3 nominations.'}, 'lastupdated': '2015-09-15 04:38:27.483000000', 'year': 2013, 'imdb': {'rating': 5.9, 'votes': 40, 'id': 3203462}, 'countries': ['Canada'], 'type': 'movie'}\n",
